{#
  The control panel shell — a vBulletin 3 AdminCP, rebuilt for 2026.
  ==================================================================

  Generic on purpose: this template knows about a header, a left-hand menu, a
  content panel and a footer, and nothing about any particular application. The
  skin lives in Resources/public/styles/admin-cp.css, which documents what is
  faithful to vB3 and what was deliberately modernised (the type scale, the lack
  of a frameset, the measured contrast).

  ── How an app uses it ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Write one thin layout in your own bundle and point every admin page at that,
  rather than having each page extend this directly — then branding and menu are
  declared once. In templates/admin/layout.html.twig in YOUR bundle:

      {% extends '@PixiekatSymfonyHelpers/admin/cp_layout.html.twig' %}
      {% set admin_nav_sections = [
        {label: 'Users', links: [{route: 'admin_users_list', label: 'All users'}]},
      ] %}
      {% block admin_brand %}My App Control Panel{% endblock %}

  (NB: the examples in this comment are illustrative text, not live code. Twig
  comments do NOT nest, so nothing in here may contain a comment-close sequence
  — a nested one ends this block early and turns the rest of the file into live
  template code, which fails with a syntax error a long way from the cause.)

  A top-level {% set %} in a child template runs before the parent renders, and
  the parent's blocks see it — which is what makes the menu declarable as data
  without this template needing to know any route names. If you would rather
  build the menu some other way (from the database, from a voter-filtered list),
  override {% block admin_nav %} instead and ignore the variable entirely.

  ── The existing seam ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  This bundle's own admin pages (ban/*, taxonomy/*) go through
  @PixiekatSymfonyHelpers/admin_layout.html.twig, which passes straight to the
  host's base.html.twig so the bundle works with no configuration. An app that
  wants those pages inside the control panel too overrides that one seam — put
  this in templates/bundles/PixiekatSymfonyHelpersBundle/admin_layout.html.twig:

      {% extends '@PixiekatSymfonyHelpers/admin/cp_layout.html.twig' %}

  That is why the shell is a NEW template rather than being poured into the seam:
  adopting it stays a decision the app makes, and the zero-config default holds.

  ── Blocks ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    admin_brand         header bar text            (default: "Control Panel")
    admin_page_title    <title> AND the panel's title bar — named once, used twice
    admin_header_links  right side of the header   (default: empty)
    admin_nav           the left menu              (default: renders admin_nav_sections)
    content             the page itself
    admin_footer        the footer line
#}
{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}{% block admin_page_title %}Control Panel{% endblock %} · {{ block('admin_brand') }}{% endblock %}

{% block stylesheets %}
  {# parent() first, so the host app's own stylesheets load and the control panel
     skin comes after them — later rules win, which is the order an app expects
     when it adds a stylesheet of its own on top of this one. #}
  {{ parent() }}
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ asset('bundles/pixiekatsymfonyhelpers/styles/admin-cp.css') }}">
{% endblock %}

{% block body %}
  {# First focusable thing on the page. With the menu ahead of the content in the
     DOM — and on a narrow screen that means literally above it — a keyboard or
     screen reader user needs one keystroke past it. #}
  <a class="skip-link" href="#main">Skip to content</a>

  <div class="vb-page">
    <div class="vb-header">
      {# <h1> here rather than on the content panel: the control panel is the
         page, and the panel title below is a section within it. That keeps the
         document outline h1 → h2 instead of starting at h2 or repeating h1. #}
      <h1>{% block admin_brand %}Control Panel{% endblock %}</h1>
      <div class="vb-header__meta">
        {% block admin_header_links %}{% endblock %}
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="vb-body">
      {% block admin_nav %}
        {#
          `only` keeps the partial's contract explicit: it receives these two
          variables and nothing else, so it cannot quietly start depending on
          something that happens to be in scope in one app and not another.
        #}
        {% include '@PixiekatSymfonyHelpers/admin/_cp_nav.html.twig' with {
          sections: admin_nav_sections|default([]),
          current_route: app.request.attributes.get('_route'),
        } only %}
      {% endblock %}

      <main id="main" class="vb-content">
        <h2 class="vb-content__title">{{ block('admin_page_title') }}</h2>

        <div class="vb-content__inner">
          {#
            Flash messages, at the top of the content panel where the eye lands
            after a redirect. role=alert for errors so they interrupt; role=status
            for the rest so they are announced politely, after the current phrase.
          #}
          {% for label, messages in app.flashes(['success', 'error', 'info', 'danger', 'warning']) %}
            {% for message in messages %}
              <p class="flash flash--{{ label }}" role="{{ label in ['error', 'danger'] ? 'alert' : 'status' }}">{{ message }}</p>
            {% endfor %}
          {% endfor %}

          {% block content %}{% endblock %}
        </div>
      </main>
    </div>

    <p class="vb-footer">{% block admin_footer %}{{ block('admin_brand') }}{% endblock %}</p>
  </div>
{% endblock %}
